  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\n- Multiple chances of rain into Friday\n\nThe last of the shower activity from the showers and storms that \nstarted moving in last evening are now along U.S.-131 and rapidly \nmoving east. Once these are gone in the next couple of hours, we \nwill stay dry until toward midnight tonight. We will see low clouds \non the backside of the low spread across the northern half of the \nforecast area late this morning and early this afternoon. They will \nthen move out by this evening.\n\nThe cold front will drop down to just south of the state to about \nthe I-80 corridor by 00z this evening. It will then slow down for a \nbit as we see a short wave eject from the Western long wave trough, \nand ride along it. A 30 knot low level jet mainly just south of the \narea will force showers to develop over roughly the south half of \nthe forecast area. Thunder chances tonight look much less likely, \nwith the sufficient instability present only near the I-94 corridor.\n\nWednesday looks to be mainly dry as we see upper ridging build over \nthe area behind the departing system Tuesday night, and the system \norganizing to move in here Thursday into Friday.\n\nThe Thursday/Friday system will be quite dynamic as we have a strong \nnorthern branch wave diving southeast from Canada, and a wave across \nthe SW U.S. lifting up to the NE and phasing with the northern \nstream system. Strong upward motion will move over the area on \nThursday ahead of the phased long wave trough. An associated 40-50 \nknot low level jet will help to focus moisture transport over the \narea Thursday, bringing better rains to the southern half of the \narea once again. Thunder chances once again look limited to the I-94 \ncorridor.\n\nShowers will continue Friday, with the focus of the rain being along \nthe lakeshore. This will occur as the upper trough moves overhead \nbriefly. The upper jet core sinks south of the state briefly, \nproviding for deep colder air and instability over the lake. The \nlake is generally sitting around 15-17C and 850 mb temps are \nforecast to drop to around 0C Friday afternoon. The better lake \neffect rain showers will tend to be earlier. We see the upper jet \ncore lift NE out of the area by later in the day. This will allow \nsubsidence to help limit the vertical development of the lake effect \nby evening. We will see quite windy conditions with 40 mph gusts \nvery plausible, especially near the lakeshore.\n\n- Dry and seasonable this weekend into early next week\n\nThe upper trough will zip away from the area late Friday, and upper \nridging will be building in quickly by Saturday. This upper ridge \nwill be slow to move out due to the amplified upper air pattern with \nthe stronger system just off of the east coast, and the closed off \nlow to the west.\n\nEventually this closed off low will weaken, and likely eject to the \nNE toward the area sometime early next week. This is likely to be \nour next chance of rain after Friday. The timing of it will depend \nhow quick or slow the upper low is to dampen and move up toward the \narea.",
